        <div id="rv-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Starting a 13,500 BTU RV Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 13,500 BTU rooftop air conditioner is the most common unit on 30-amp travel trailers and many motorhomes. Once running it typically draws around 1,300 to 1,800 watts depending on outdoor temperature. Starting is the problem. The compressor can pull a brief inrush well above 3,000 watts, and it is highest on hot afternoons, after a short off cycle, or when the generator voltage sags under other loads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding the Rest of the Trailer</h3>

          <p>
            Add the rest of a typical trailer: a converter charging the batteries at 300 to 600 watts, the refrigerator on electric at 300 to 400, lights, a water pump and a television. While the AC runs you are near 2,500 to 3,000 watts, which is fine on 3,500 running watts. When the compressor cycles back on, the generator has to supply the AC inrush on top of everything else for a second or two. A 4,500 starting watt inverter covers that in most conditions. A 3,500 starting watt inverter often does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fit a Soft Starter</h3>

          <p>
            The single best upgrade for any RV generator is a soft starter on the air conditioner. It ramps the compressor up gradually, and soft start makers advertise large cuts in inrush current. Owners commonly report that a soft-started 13,500 BTU unit runs on generators as small as 2,200 to 3,000 watts. On a 4500 watt inverter, a soft starter turns a borderline start into a non-event and lets you run the microwave without shutting the AC off first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Larger and Dual Air Conditioners</h3>

          <p>
            A 15,000 BTU unit draws more, often 1,700 to 2,000 running watts, and is a firmer case for 4,500 starting watts plus a soft starter. Two air conditioners on a 50-amp rig are beyond this class entirely; see our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> and the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generators with a 50 amp outlet</a>. Our article on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-an-air-conditioner/">whether a power station can run an air conditioner</a> explains why battery-only setups struggle with this load.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Turn the AC on last and give it a minute.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Start the generator, let it warm up, then switch on the air conditioner before the microwave or water heater element. If the compressor short-cycles, wait three minutes before restarting it; a compressor restarting against high pressure is the hardest start there is.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="window-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. A Window AC Plus the Refrigerator at Home
          </h2>

          <p>
            For home backup the 4000 watt class is the smallest size that keeps one room cool during a summer outage without giving up the refrigerator. A 10,000 BTU window air conditioner typically runs at 1,000 to 1,200 watts and surges to 2,200 to 3,000 watts. A refrigerator runs at about 700 and surges to 2,200. Add 400 watts of lights, a router and a television, and the running total is around 2,100 to 2,300 watts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where 3500 Watts Falls Short</h3>

          <p>
            The starting side is where a 3500W generator gets uncomfortable. If the refrigerator and lights are running at about 1,100 watts and the window unit starts, the generator has to supply up to about 4,100 watts for a moment. That is inside 4,500 starting watts, and outside the 3,500 to 3,600 surge of a compact inverter. If both compressors try to start at once, which happens after a restart, even 4,500 watts is tight; plug the air conditioner in after the refrigerator has settled.
          </p>

