        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 4000 watt generator run a 13,500 BTU RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. A 13,500 BTU unit runs at roughly 1,300 to 1,800 watts but surges well above 3,000 watts when starting. A generator with 4,500 starting watts handles that in most conditions with the refrigerator and lights running. A 4,000 peak watt unit is borderline on hot days or at altitude; a soft starter fixes it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 4000 watt generator run in a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It runs a refrigerator, a 10,000 BTU window air conditioner, lights, a router and a television together, or in winter a refrigerator, gas furnace blower, lights and a 1/2 horsepower sump pump. It will not run central air, electric heat, an electric water heater or an electric range. Stagger motor starts to stay within the surge rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 4500 watt generator the same as a 4000 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are the same class. Model names usually quote starting watts, and most so-called 4000 watt generators are sold as 4500 watt units with 3,500 to 3,800 running watts. The Westinghouse iGen4000DFc is rated 4,000 peak and 3,300 running; the Champion 201319 and WEN DF450i are rated 4,500 starting and 3,500 running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a 4500 watt inverter generator weigh?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enclosed 4500 watt inverters are typically listed at around 90 to 100 pounds, which is why most come with wheels and a telescoping handle. Open-frame inverters can be lighter; Champion lists the 201320 at 78 pounds. Compact inverters below this class weigh around 50 pounds but give up the surge an air conditioner needs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 4000 watt generator run a 30 amp RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A 30 amp RV outlet, the TT-30R, is rated for 3,600 watts, which matches the running capacity of this class. Four of our five picks have a TT-30R built in. You can run the air conditioner, refrigerator, converter and lights, but avoid running the microwave and water heater element together with the AC.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 240 volt generator for a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most panel interlock kits and whole-panel inlet setups, yes. They expect a 120/240 volt L14-30R outlet so the generator can feed both legs of the panel. Some small transfer switches for a few circuits work with 120 volt L5-30R outlets. Check your switch before buying; the WEN DF472X is our 240 volt pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 4500 watt generator run on a tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on tank size and load. Rated runtimes at 25 percent load range from about 7 hours on the Westinghouse iGen4000DFc to 22 hours on the Champion 201320. With an air conditioner running, expect roughly half the rated figure. Propane extends runtime considerably, and a 20 pound cylinder often outlasts a small gasoline tank.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 4000 label for 4,000 running watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost nothing in this class runs 4,000 watts continuously. The label is the starting rating, and running watts sit between 3,300 and 3,800. Plan your continuous load against the running figure and keep it under about 80 percent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the soft starter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A soft starter on the RV air conditioner is the cheapest generator upgrade you can make. Without one, a 13,500 BTU unit on a hot afternoon can trip even a 4,500 watt inverter, especially at altitude or on propane.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring weight until the first trip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most enclosed 4500 watt inverters weigh 90 to 100 pounds. Plan how you will lift it into the truck or onto a carrier, and how you will roll it from storage to a safe run spot, before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a 240V transfer switch into a 120V generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A TT-30R or 5-20R outlet cannot feed both legs of a house panel. If you want to power hardwired circuits through an interlock or transfer switch, you need an L14-30R generator like the WEN DF472X.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it next to the camper door</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carbon monoxide from a generator beside an RV door or window can build up inside quickly. Point the exhaust away, keep distance, run a CO alarm inside, and prefer a model with an automatic CO shutoff.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

